  "raw_text": "Step 1: Recognize that for any \\(x\\) in the domain of \\(f(x)\\), \\(\\arccos x\\) and \\(\\arcsin x\\) are defined and their sum is \\(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\).\n\nStep 2: Use the identity \\(\\arccos x + \\arcsin x = \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\) to express \\(\\arccos x\\) in terms of \\(\\arcsin x\\): \\(\\arccos x = \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\arcsin x\\).\n\nStep 3: Substitute \\(\\arccos x = \\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\arcsin x\\) into the function \\(f(x)\\):\n\\[f(x) = \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2} - \\arcsin x\\right)^2 + (\\arcsin x)^2.\\]\n\nStep 4: Expand the squared term:\n\\[f(x) = \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)^2 - \\pi \\arcsin x + (\\arcsin x)^2 + (\\arcsin x)^2.\\]\n\nStep 5: Combine like terms:\n\\[f(x) = \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)^2 - \\pi \\arcsin x + 2(\\arcsin x)^2.\\]\n\nStep 6: Let \\(y = \\arcsin x\\). Since \\(\\arcsin x\\) ranges from \\(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\) to \\(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\), \\(y\\) also ranges from \\(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\) to \\(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\). The function becomes:\n\\[f(y) = \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)^2 - \\pi y + 2y^2.\\]\n\nStep 7: Identify the quadratic function \\(f(y) = 2y^2 - \\pi y + \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)^2\\). This is a parabola opening upwards (since the coefficient of \\(y^2\\) is positive).\n\nStep 8: Find the vertex of the parabola to determine its minimum value. The vertex form for a parabola \\(ay^2 + by + c\\) has its vertex at \\(y = -\\frac{b}{2a}\\). Here, \\(a = 2\\) and \\(b = -\\pi\\), so:\n\\[y = \\frac{\\pi}{4}.\\]\n\nStep 9: Substitute \\(y = \\frac{\\pi}{4}\\) back into the function to find the minimum value:\n\\[f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = 2\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right)^2 - \\pi \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{4}\\right) + \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)^2 = 2 \\cdot \\frac{\\pi^2}{16} - \\frac{\\pi^2}{4} + \\frac{\\pi^2}{4} = \\frac{\\pi^2}{8}.\\]\n\nStep 10: Determine the maximum value of \\(f(y)\\) over the interval \\([- \\frac{\\pi}{2}, \\frac{\\pi}{2}]\\). Evaluate \\(f(y)\\) at the endpoints:\n\\[f\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = 2\\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)^2 - \\pi \\left(-\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) + \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)^2 = 2 \\cdot \\frac{\\pi^2}{4} + \\frac{\\pi^2}{2} + \\frac{\\pi^2}{4} = \\frac{3\\pi^2}{2},\\]\n\\[f\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = 2\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)^2 - \\pi \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right) + \\left(\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\right)^2 = 2 \\cdot \\frac{\\pi^2}{4} - \\frac{\\pi^2}{2} + \\frac{\\pi^2}{4} = \\frac{3\\pi^2}{2}.\\]\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{\\left[\\frac{\\pi^2}{8}, \\frac{3\\pi^2}{2}\\right]}<|im_end|>",
